			<div class="content" id="post-content-2039846">
									

<p>Here are some tone problems I have.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Kind&nbsp;of&nbsp;a&nbsp;lot</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-I'll&nbsp;leave&nbsp;comments&nbsp;below&nbsp;some&nbsp;if&nbsp;they&nbsp;need&nbsp;some&nbsp;specification</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Implanted Foundation agents in major observatories are to contain footage or images of SCP-2399.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just wanted to say that you would think the Foundation has their own observatory for this, instead of having to use other agencies at the risk of exposing people</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and make all attempts to hinder that process should SCP-2399 reach a minimum of 75% completion</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This could use rephrasing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2399 has been observed releasing a multitude of small repair drones in efforts to repair the damage it has taken.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You'd think that they would go into detail about these drones, but nope, don't even know what they look like</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite its damaged state, SCP-2399 is a threat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Many SCPs are a threat (to what, humanity? Normality?) and this isn't necessary to mention. Just say if its damaged state had affected its performance or something else</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the large difference in technological advancement between the creator of SCP-2399 and our own, for all intents and purposes, SCP-2399 is indestructible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Good to see us admitting defeat, even though we don't fully understand it :|</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, in theory SCP-2399 might be left vulnerable by a powerful enough electromagnetic pulse. Unfortunately, this technology does not yet exist.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Powerful enough electromagnetic pulse&quot; is something you would say in speech, not an article. And didn't you just say this thing was indestructible? It can be assumed that the Foundation would be working on something to counter this sort of thing, since they're all scientists and have the money</p>
<blockquote>
<p>To our surprise, a magnificent change has occured!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, Galileo found Jupiter's moons and the sun's spots in the 16th century. I'm just not sure that they had enough tech to see Jupiter's <em>surface</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>current information maintains that Jupiter has only ever had 4 moons</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Has only 4 moons</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Command lost contact with BARRIER Unit 45&#160;15 seconds after initial discharge, with video observation showing SCP-2399 [DATA EXPUNGED] the resulting spacial anomaly originating in ████-██-████-█ [DATA EXPUNGED] the termination of BARRIER Unit 45 by BARRIER Units 44, 51, and 55.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>:/ I hope this bad expungment pays off</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After the events of ██/██/██, it was decided that necessary force would be authorized to destroy or incapacitate SCP-2399.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How did we hide the launching, why would we even nuclear warfare, this sounded really reckless.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, eh. Giant doomsday device, impending doom oh no, not so sure about the facts and decisions made, sorry.</p>

							</div>

								
					
