			<div class="content" id="post-content-2039847">
									

<p>I appreciate the comments, but I don't want you to think I did things without thinking about them.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;So&nbsp;some&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;No&nbsp;more&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Just wanted to say that you would think the Foundation has their own observatory for this, instead of having to use other agencies at the risk of exposing people</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They undoubtedly do. But they're not the only ones with big telescopes. There are obviously other observatories that could view it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You'd think that they would go into detail about these drones, but nope, don't even know what they look like</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I did that in one iteration, but decided to leave it out when it got too wordy. The article doesn't at all describe SCP-2399 either.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Many SCPs are a threat (to what, humanity? Normality?) and this isn't necessary to mention. Just say if its damaged state had affected its performance or something else</p>
</blockquote>
<p>*shrugs* Fair enough. This could've probably been left out. It's a leftover from when that was a longer sentence, and nobody had said anything about it yet.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Good to see us admitting defeat, even though we don't fully understand it :|</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The addendums go into detail on why they consider it indestructible.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Powerful enough electromagnetic pulse&quot; is something you would say in speech, not an article. And didn't you just say this thing was indestructible? It can be assumed that the Foundation would be working on something to counter this sort of thing, since they're all scientists and have the money</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'll concede the first part. But the rest is sort of the point. We've theorized as to how we could blow it up, but we don't have the technology to do it. As for the last bit&#8230; that's elaborated on in the Director's note.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Yeah, Galileo found Jupiter's moons and the sun's spots in the 16th century. I'm just not sure that they had enough tech to see Jupiter's surface.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A nitpick, but it was Cassini. He was the first to observe the Great Red Spot, in the 17th century.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Has only 4 moons</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Necessary to mention, since the addendums go into how it hasn't always had 4.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>:/ I hope this bad expungment pays off</p>
</blockquote>
<p>*shrugs again* It's your opinion, you're more than entitled to it. Nobody else had mentioned it yet, so it got left in.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How did we hide the launching, why would we even nuclear warfare, this sounded really reckless.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do we do anything that some of these things require? You don't think that if we found a doomsday machine in space, world leaders wouldn't be jumping on the wagon to try and blow it up?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Thanks again for the thoughts, though. I won't be getting any better at this without input. It's definitely valued. Much appreciated!
							</div>

								
